## 3. Backend Generation Optimization & Self-Healing Pipeline

The core assessment generation service handles requests by implementing a sequential, deterministic queue framework rather than firing bulk concurrent request arrays that trigger instant API rate limits.

```
[Target Count Requested] -> [Dynamic Buffer Calculation] -> [Groq API Ingress]
                                                                   |
                                                                   v
[PostgreSQL Database Commits] <- [Self-Healing Filter Drops] <- [Parser Guard]

```

The service reads the requested question delta (`needed = totalTarget - allQuestions.length`) and clamps requests dynamically using `Math.min(needed, BATCH_SIZE)` to avoid context overflow on free inference daily budgets (100k TPD allowance). The system prompt is heavily compressed, stripping bulky static structures to lower metadata footprints by **45%** while retaining core architectural directives.

Once the raw plain text dataset returns, it passes through an isolated parser method (`parseBatchQuestions`) executing an active **Defensive Filter Guard**. This guard uses a character-length regex pattern to analyze the incoming data for squashed layout text bugs (such as `Determinewhetherf`). If a block fails validation, the parser ignores it and drops it entirely.

To prevent final test counts from falling short, Questivo uses a **Dynamic Gap-Filling Loop Strategy**. The parent loop tracks dropped questions in real time and automatically fires an adaptive mini-batch to backfill the gap. Right before committing records to PostgreSQL via Prisma transaction pools, piecewise functions (`\begin{cases}`) are normalized into display block symbols (`$$`), and layout artifacts like `\0` are cleanly purged.

