## 4. Frontend Isolated Parsing & Typographic Rendering Bridge

Once raw data fields are saved to the database, they must be rendered on the screen without text layout degradation. If text strings are translated globally, it fragments descriptive labels. To bypass this, the client interface architecture uses a custom rendering pipeline.

The UI wraps question statements, multi-choice grids, resume analytics logs, and interview question breakdowns inside a **`SafeMathRenderer`** component, which handles data streams using an isolation wrapper algorithm named **`applyToTextOnly`**.

```
                        [Incoming Payload String]
                                    |
                    +---------------+---------------+
                    |                               |
          [Math Regions ($$, $)]           [Pure Text Nodes]
                    |                               |
                    v                               v
             (Bypass Loop)               (Token Boundary Scanner)
                    |                               |
                    |                      Wraps plain operators 
                    |                      (^, _, =, pm) into $ $
                    |                               |
                    +---------------+---------------+
                                    |
                                    v
                        [Flawless Vector Render]

```

The rendering wrapper reads string inputs character-by-character to locate pre-existing markdown boundaries (`$$` and `$`) and completely isolates them from mutation loops to prevent double-wrapping errors. The matching scanner then processes only the plain text regions, dividing them into micro-token strings.

The engine analyzes each token for core math patterns (`\frac`, `\sqrt`, `\langle`) as well as physics signatures (`\mu_0`, `\Delta`, `C_V`, `MR^2`). If any are found, the scanner strips layout noise, handles messy punctuation edge-cases (like trailing commas or trailing raw `$` signs), and wraps the raw expression inside crisp inline boundaries (`$ ... $`). This structural contract ensures that whether a user is looking at a live Calculus problem or a complex rotational mechanics question, the interface displays flawless, scalable typographic vectors without any performance bottlenecks.

